import unicodedata, os

BODY = """Subject: The Grid Wire, Vol. 14 -- Morning Edition, August 26, 2026

{salutation}

Vol. 14 is attached. Window is August 19 (Vol 13 baseline) through this morning's pre-open. Twenty-two pages. Three developments carry the cut.

1. THE BEHIND-THE-METER EXEMPTION IS NARROWER THAN WE HAVE BEEN CARRYING IT.

At the August 20 PUCT open meeting, the Project 58481 requirements were stated to apply to all loads at or above 75 MW regardless of load type, regardless of co-location, and regardless of whether the load sits behind the meter or in front of it. We have carried off-grid and BTM configurations as a regulatory moat since the August 7 cut. As a categorical claim that is now falsified. What survives is real but narrower: BTM still avoids the interconnection queue, the study cycle, and the energization pause. It does not avoid disclosure, verification, audit, penalties, or the $50,000/MW financial requirement payable at Interconnection Agreement execution.

Also from that meeting: the PUCT granted all three ERCOT good-cause exceptions under Project 59142. ERCOT may now conditionally classify Batch 0 loads by August 31 instead of the missed August 7 deadline, conditionally classified base loads may enter the August 1 and November 1 QSA, and developers get a 24-day cure window on dynamic-data deficiencies. Separately, ERCOT told the Commission it has paused all approvals to energize unenergized large-load data-center and crypto facilities until Batch 0 eligibility verification is complete. Verification runs RFIs through September, additional RFIs and cure October to November, and an eligibility report December 10. Practical consequence: there is currently no energization date in ERCOT that anyone can underwrite.

One more line worth flagging, because it is the most underrated item in the rulemaking: ERCOT may reallocate transmission capacity if a project misses a required milestone by 24 months. That converts a queue position from a free option into a use-it-or-lose-it lease. It is the correct way to structure a scarce physical right and it destroys the value of speculative queue inventory.

2. A 1.5 GW ISLANDED CAMPUS WAS ANNOUNCED IN REEVES COUNTY ON AUGUST 20.

Prometheus Hyperscale and Istmo Energy announced the Pecos Project: 1.5 GW Phase 1 with a development path to 2.5 GW, islanded from ERCOT with no initial interconnection, pipeline natural gas at the start, moving to an approximate 85 percent gas / 15 percent ethane baseline once Istmo's co-located fractionation facility enters service in Q1 2028, closed-loop cooling, first power targeted 2027 on modular deployments. No offtaker named. Acreage, capex, financing, and equipment supplier all undisclosed.

That makes four disclosed gigawatt-class configurations in Reeves County: Chevron Energy Forge One Project Kilby at roughly 2.67 GW with a 20-year Microsoft PPA and TPL surface plus exclusive aquifer water sourcing; PowerBridge Alpha Digital on roughly 3,400 LandBridge acres at up to 2 GW with the GINR filed and long-lead equipment ordered; Prometheus at 1.5 to 2.5 GW; and the NRG-LandBridge 1,100 MW option contingent on a PPA. Roughly 7.3 GW of disclosed generation intent in one county. Only Kilby has a named investment-grade counterparty and a disclosed tenor, so none of the other three supports a Kilby-derived dollar-per-MW mark. Contracted, optioned, announced, and conditional are four different assets.

3. THE 765 kV BUILD HAS BECOME A POLITICAL QUESTION, AND THE INTERVENORS ARE ARGUING OUR THESIS.

At the August 21 hearing the Commission consolidated Dockets 59029 (Longshore Switch to Drill Hole Switch, 160 miles, terminus near the Culberson-Reeves line) and 59315 (Dinosaur to Longshore) on the issue of need only. No final decision. Two commissioners were absent. Both segments plus the southern Howard to Solstice line are back on the August 28 agenda.

The record around it is deteriorating for Oncor. SOAH judges found Oncor out of compliance with the pre-filing public-meeting requirement after adding route links that affected roughly 1,650 additional owners with no notice, and cited the Fifteenth Court of Appeals holding in PUC v. City of Denton that an agency may not disregard its own rules. In the Bell County East to Big Hill docket the judges recommended rejection, citing intervenor evidence that new and planned Permian gas generation could reduce or eliminate the need for the import path. Patrick and Schwertner have called for denial of all pending applications; 31 House members filed a letter on August 21 to the same effect.

Note what the winning intervenor argument actually is. It is that in-basin gas generation substitutes for imported power. If the Commission accepts it, the state has effectively endorsed the off-grid, gas-at-the-wellhead configuration that Kilby, Alpha Digital, Pembrook and now Prometheus are all building. The transmission denial case and the powered-land case are the same case.

WATER, AND THE TWO THINGS WE STILL DO NOT KNOW.

The Middle Pecos GCD met August 17 on the CRMWD and La Escalera Capitan Reef export permits. The outcome is still not public. No order, no permit, nothing on the district's notice page. It remains the highest-information undisclosed datapoint in the district and I am carrying it open for the third cut running.

Reeves County GCD historic-use deadline is September 1. Six days. No published extension.

Reminder on discipline here: Middle Pecos and Reeves are separate districts with separate rules and separate deadlines. Nothing in the Capitan Reef export question bears on Reeves seniority. Any comp built across the two is invalid.

The water comp ladder still has no completed arm's-length transaction on it. TPL conveyed surface acreage to Chevron for cash plus exclusive aquifer-derived water sourcing rights and disclosed neither the cash nor the water price. That is the structural problem: the transactions that would price the scarcest input in the stack are being done inside vertically integrated land-and-water platforms where the water is bundled and never separately marked. The first standalone, priced, dated acre-foot transaction in Reeves or Pecos reprices every powered-land site in the basin at once.

GAS EGRESS, AND A REAL CHALLENGE TO OUR FUEL-COST LEG.

WhiteWater and partners took FID on the Solitude Pipeline System on August 17: two 48-inch lines from the Permian to Katy, 2.25 Bcf/d in late 2029 and another 2.25 Bcf/d in 2030, roughly 4.5 Bcf/d total, expandable, backed by long-term firm transportation with predominantly investment-grade shippers. WhiteWater 50 percent, Devon 25, MPLX 10, Diamondback 7.5, Western Midstream 7.5. Capex and tariff undisclosed.

Meanwhile NGI's Waha averaged about $1.64 in July and is averaging just over $2.00 so far in August. AEGIS had cash between roughly $1.80 and $2.23 for the week to August 14 with the forward basis curve barely moving. Intrastate eastward flows are up about 1.4 Bcf/d year over year. For context, Waha averaged about minus $2.19 in the first half of 2026 with a record minus $10.03 on April 24.

I want to be direct about what this does to us. The negative-basis era was the cleanest evidence that molecules had no route to market and therefore had to be consumed where they sat. Above $2.00, with 4.5 Bcf/d of additional egress now sanctioned, the in-basin power case can no longer rest on free gas. It has to rest on the delivered cost of firm molecules at the meter versus the delivered cost of grid power under the 58481 regime. That is a narrower spread, and it is the honest one to underwrite. The single largest falsification risk to the whole powered-land thesis moved this week from demand to fuel-cost convergence.

PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS ARE STILL THE STRONGEST LEG.

GE Vernova closed Q2 with 116 GW of gas backlog plus slot reservations, up from 100 GW in Q1 and 83 GW at the end of 2025, targeting at least 125 GW by year end. Of the 116 GW, only 53 GW is firm equipment backlog; 63 GW is paid slot reservations. A heavy-duty order placed today delivers in 2031, confirmed on the July 22 call. Siemens carries 69 GW firm with no reservations mixed in. Mitsubishi reported 35 GW of large-frame backlog on August 6, up from 23 GW, with quarter bookings delivering 2028 to 2030 and the CFO saying they are being selective. Generator step-up transformers run 144 weeks and large power transformers 128 weeks against a 30 percent supply deficit through 2027.

The read on the turbine makers is not the headline number. Adding the three gets you 220 GW and it is meaningless, because 63 GW of the GE Vernova figure is paid optionality rather than committed steel. The manufacturers have converted a supply shortage into a reservation-fee business, collecting cash today on capacity they may never build, with the schedule risk sitting on the developer. That is the same structure as a queue position and it will be revalued the same way. Whoever holds a firm 2027 or 2028 delivery slot holds the scarcest paper in the stack, and there is still no public price for it.

CREDIT AND RATES.

CoreWeave closed its DDTL 5.5 on August 10: $2.6 billion, JPMorgan and MUFG, SOFR plus 550, roughly five years, Ba2/BB+, oversubscribed, structured explicitly to finance shorter-dated customer contracts. Against the DDTL 4.0 template at SOFR plus 225, that is 325 basis points of tenor premium at the same borrower. The market is now charging for contract tenor by itself, which is the same variable that makes a 20-year take-or-pay PPA the most valuable document in the stack.

CoreWeave's Q2 balance sheet: about $35 billion of total debt, $640 million of interest expense in the quarter and guided higher, debt to equity 8.94 times, net debt to EBITDA 10.75 times, against a $104.2 billion backlog of which roughly half does not convert to revenue for four or more years. Nebius is funding the same physical assets on roughly $9 billion of 2026 customer prepayments covering 50 to 60 percent of capex on its landmark deals, with about 70 percent of signed deals carrying prepayments. That is negative-cost capital with no covenant, no DSCR test and no refinancing wall, and it appears on no spread ladder anywhere.

On rates: Treasury announced on August 19 that it would at least double repurchases of 10, 20 and 30-year debt, and the long end went up anyway. The 30-year printed above 5.3 percent on August 20, a 19-year high, then eased to 5.2004 percent by Tuesday's close on a separate report that Bessent could tap the roughly $1 trillion Treasury General Account to fund buybacks. Cited drivers of the term premium include AI-related corporate issuance competing for duration. Read that twice. The financed layer of the AI stack is now large enough to raise its own discount rate, and none of it touches the price of an acre.

TONIGHT.

Nvidia reports FQ2 2027 after the close, call at 5:00 PM ET. Sell-side consensus is roughly $91.9 to $92.2 billion in revenue against company guidance of about $91.0 billion plus or minus 2 percent, so an in-line print is not a beat against management's own number. I am not reading the revenue line first. I am reading, in order: the land, power and shell guarantee line last disclosed at $3.5 billion; the aggregate financing guarantees including the Ports-Pike Ohio structure finalized August 17 at a $105 billion cap, down about 58 percent from the roughly $250 billion discussed in July; supply commitments against the $119.0 billion Q1 figure; multi-year cloud commitments against $30.0 billion; and any new off-balance-sheet disclosure tied to the reported $500 billion third-party financing pool. If those lines are flat or lower, the vendor most central to the cycle is stepping back from underwriting the physical layer, and our wedge argument weakens.

THE CLOCKS.

Reeves GCD historic use, 6 days. ERCOT conditional classification, 5 days. Warsh at Jackson Hole and the PUCT 765 kV agenda, both 2 days. Batch Zero study start September 2, uncertain. DDTL 3.0 cure expiry, 63 days. Batch 0 eligibility report, 106 days. Batch Zero study deadline April 9, 2027, 226 days.

WHERE I COME OUT.

The thesis holds and its center of gravity moved. Physical inputs got scarcer or more expensive to demonstrate on turbines, transformers, water, and documented site control. Physical inputs lost ground on two counts: the BTM exemption is narrower than we carried it, and Waha above $2.00 with sanctioned egress narrows the free-fuel advantage. The financed layer lost on every count: no underwritable energization date, a long end that will not stay down under an administrative bid, and issuance that is now raising its own cost of capital. Net, the strongest leg has rotated from cheap stranded gas to documented, permitted, water-attached, gas-adjacent site control. That is a better leg to stand on, and it is a narrower one.

Fee stack correction of record, restated: per 16 TAC section 25.194 and the 58481 discussion, it is $50,000/MW plus study fees plus staged security. The $100,000 plus $100,000 stack in the July cuts is superseded and should not be cited externally.

Sources, undisclosed items, and the full falsification conditions are in the PDF.

Andrea

Andrea Himmel
LAND
The Grid Wire
"""

SUBS = {"\u2014":"--","\u2013":"-","\u2018":"'","\u2019":"'","\u201c":'"',"\u201d":'"',
        "\u00b7":"-","\u2026":"...","\u00a0":" ","\u2212":"-","\u00ad":"","\u2032":"'","\u2033":'"'}

def norm(s):
    for k,v in SUBS.items():
        s = s.replace(k,v)
    s = unicodedata.normalize("NFKD", s).encode("ascii","ignore").decode("ascii")
    assert all(ord(c) < 128 for c in s)
    return s

os.makedirs("/mnt/user-data/outputs", exist_ok=True)
for who, sal, fn in [("Mel","Mel,","Grid_Wire_Vol14_EMAIL_Mel.txt"),
                     ("Mark","Mark,","Grid_Wire_Vol14_EMAIL_Mark.txt")]:
    out = norm(BODY.format(salutation=sal))
    p = f"/mnt/user-data/outputs/{fn}"
    open(p,"w").write(out)
    print(who, p, len(out))
